1) Starting the full browser
----------------------------

The pages are drawn by Chrome, running out of sight on the computer
that runs the desktop's server. Start the server with:

  node serve.mjs --browser

and open the desktop as usual (http://localhost:8371/). The server
looks for, in this order: Chrome for Testing or Chromium (as installed
by Playwright or Puppeteer), then Chromium, Google Chrome, Microsoft
Edge or Brave. To choose one:

  node serve.mjs --browser=/path/to/chrome

Chrome for Testing or Chromium is best: only they can send pages'
sound to the desktop (Google Chrome no longer allows the small add-on
that does it). To get Chrome for Testing:

  npx @puppeteer/browsers install chrome@stable

The server says which browser it found, and whether it has sound.

!Browse's pages use a profile of their own, kept in ~/.riscos-browse
on that computer, so you stay logged in to sites between sessions.
Another folder:

  node serve.mjs --browser --browser-profile ~/somewhere-else


2) Browsing
-----------

Click SELECT on the !Browse icon on the icon bar for a window. Type an
address in the URL bar and press Return. Anything that isn't an
address (words with spaces, say) is looked up with the search site
from Choices.

The buttons along the top are:

  Home      your home page (Choices)
  Back      the page before
  Reload    this page again (ADJUST: without anything kept from before)
  Stop      stop fetching
  Forward   forward again
  Add       add this page to your hotlist
  Hotlist   choose a page from your hotlist

The arrow at the right of the URL bar lists the pages you've visited.
The globe spins while a page is fetched. The status bar says what's
happening and, when the pointer is over a link, where it goes.

The mouse works on pages as you'd expect: SELECT clicks, drags and
selects text, the wheel scrolls. ADJUST on a link opens it in a new
tab behind this one. MENU gives !Browse's menu, not the page's.

The window's scroll bars follow the page: drag them to scroll it.
The window can be made any size, and the page fits it.

A page's drop-down lists open as RISC OS menus. Its messages ("Are
you sure?") are RISC OS error boxes, and a page asking you to type
something gets a box to type into.

Keys go to the page, except these:

  F3                save the page (HTML)
  F4                find text in the page
  F5                reload
  Ctrl-L            the URL bar
  Ctrl-T            a new tab
  Ctrl-W            close the tab
  Ctrl-N            a new window
  Alt-Left/Right    back / forward

(On a Mac, Cmd instead of Ctrl.) Ctrl-C and Ctrl-X copy the page's
selected text to your computer's clipboard, and Ctrl-V pastes into
the page.

Zoom: Display > Zoom on the menu makes the page bigger or smaller
(the status bar shows how much).


3) Tabs and windows
-------------------

A window can hold several pages, in tabs. When there's more than one,
a tab bar appears under the URL bar: click SELECT on a tab to show its
page, ADJUST to close it; + opens a new tab. The Tabs menu does the
same. Pages that open new windows ("pop-ups", and links meant for a
new window) open as tabs.

Closing the window closes all its tabs. (ADJUST on the close icon
closes just the tab being shown.)


4) Files: downloads, uploads, saving and printing
-------------------------------------------------

Downloads: when a page offers a file, a Save box appears straight
away. Drag its icon to a Filer window (or type a full name and click
Save): the file is saved there when it has arrived. The status bar
shows how it's going. Close the Save box to give up the download.

File names become RISC OS ones: "notes.txt" is saved as notes/txt, of
type Text, "photo.png" as photo/png, a PNG.

Uploads: when a page asks for a file ("Choose file"), a box appears:
drag the file from a Filer window into it. You can also drag files
from the Filer straight onto a page that takes them. RISC OS names
become names the page understands: photo/png is sent as photo.png.

From the File menu:

  Save              the page's HTML (a file of type HTML)
  Save location     a URI file with the page's address
  Print to PDF      the page as a PDF file

Double-click a URI file (or an ANT URL file) to open its page in
!Browse, or drag it to the !Browse icon or a !Browse window.

Other programs open web addresses in !Browse with the command

  *URLOpen_http <address>

- !Bookworm does, when you follow a link to the web in a manual.


5) The hotlist, history and Choices
-----------------------------------

The Hotlist button, and Hotlist on the menu, show your hotlist:
double-click a page to show it (ADJUST keeps the list open). Hotlist >
Add this page adds the page being shown; Remove page takes one off.

Navigate > History, or the arrow on the URL bar, lists the pages
you've visited, most recent first.

Choices... on the icon bar menu sets:

  Home page             the page new windows start with
  Search with           the site that looks up words typed in the URL
                        bar (%s stands for the words)
  Play sounds           whether pages' sound plays
  Always show tab bar   even with only one page in a window

Set uses them until you quit; Save keeps them. They're kept, with the
hotlist, in the Choices directory as Browse, and the history as
BrowseHist.


6) Without the engine
---------------------

When the server wasn't started with --browser, or the desktop comes
from another web server, or you're using it from another computer on
the network (node serve.mjs --lan), !Browse still works, but shows
pages inside the desktop's own page, in a frame. Many sites don't
allow that (most big ones): !Browse then says so, with a button to
open the site in your own browser. Finding text and zoom need the
engine, and Back and Forward only know the addresses you typed or
chose in !Browse.


7) Privacy and safety
---------------------

The engine only answers the computer it runs on: other computers on
the network, and web sites, can't use it, even with --lan. It never
opens a debugging port. Pages run in Chrome, separate from the
desktop, so a page can't read the desktop's disc or settings; and its
profile is its own, separate from your usual browser's. !Browse only
opens web addresses (http:, https:), never files on the computer.


8) Troubleshooting
------------------

"No Chrome, Chromium or Edge was found": install one (see 1) or give
its path with --browser=/path/to/chrome.

No sound: the server was using Google Chrome or Edge; use Chrome for
Testing or Chromium. Sound is also off if Display > Sound isn't
ticked.

"The browser engine stopped" or "The connection to the browser engine
was lost": the server, or Chrome, stopped. Start the server again and
reload the page to carry on.

A page looks wrong or stops working: Reload with ADJUST. For anything
!Browse can't do, Utilities > Open in your own browser.
